  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a translational pipeline for matching cancer drugs to patients. It starts with large patient cohorts and large collections of cell lines, colored to distinguish responders from non-responders. By comparing these groups with genomics and transcriptomics, researchers identify a drug-response signature or specific genetic alteration linked to treatment benefit. Isogenic cell lines appear to be used to validate these candidate biomarkers in a controlled setting. Once a predictive signature is established, it can guide precision therapies for future patients.\n\nThe lower half shows a more individualized route beginning with a single patient tumor. Material from that patient is used to create experimental models, including mouse xenografts (PDX) and cultured organoid-like systems labeled PDCL or HCCO. These patient-derived models undergo large drug screens, again supported by genomic and transcriptomic profiling, to find compounds that work against that specific tumor. The output is a personalized therapy choice for the individual patient.\n\nOverall, the image contrasts population-based biomarker discovery with patient-specific functional testing, showing how both approaches use molecular profiling and model systems to move from tumor samples to more effective treatment selection.",
